        </div>
        <p>
          The diagram on the left shows Seishinkai's emphasis.<br />
          Elsewhere, the competitive-sport side tends to carry the greatest weight.<br />
          Seishinkai doesn't reject competitive sport, but we place our emphasis based on what matters most for each age group.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="detail-page__heading">About Seishinkai's Tournament</h2>
      <p>
        Seishinkai holds one tournament per year. Think of it as a combination of a school athletic meet and a cultural festival — an opportunity to showcase everyday training.<br />
        Sparring broadly falls into three types: the "stop-before-contact" style used in high school federation and national athletic meet rules (where the technique is stopped just short of the opponent), full-contact sparring where strikes actually land (upper-level punches to the head are prohibited), and Seishinkai's own style — full protective-gear sparring, which is uncommon in Hokkaido.<br />
        Participants wear a mask, body protector, and hand/foot supporters, and strikes are allowed to land. Because of the protective gear, the chance of injury is lower than under other rule sets even when strikes connect.<br />
        Experiencing the sting of a strike, and the impact of being struck, without real injury, can help build resilience against bullying. Karate also has a martial-arts side, and in recent years the ability to react instinctively and evade danger has become an increasingly valuable skill.<br />
        Various sports competitions hold matches almost every month during their season, so training centers around preparing for those. Seishinkai, on the other hand, only holds its own in-house tournament, so day-to-day training centers on "physical-fitness karate" — basics and kata that build physical function and support healthy growth.
      </p>

